item.text:
The 2022 economic record is mixed: reform intent and some pro-poor, market-opening steps are real, but so are severe inflation, debt stress, conflict drag, and limited evidence on corruption-control and appointment quality.

**Reused evidence**
- None beyond the local structured priors; I did not find a prior source item that added a direct 5B-specific claim worth reusing.

**New evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Visit Discusses Reform Plans and Economic Developments in Ethiopia","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-21","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/21/pr22216-ethiopia-imf-staff-visit-discusses-reform-plans-and-economic-developments-in-ethiopia","claim":"The IMF said multiple shocks had dampened growth, further increased inflation, and created fiscal and external pressures; it also said the authorities aimed to address macroeconomic imbalances and stay committed to structural reforms supporting a transition to private-sector-led growth.","locator":"lines 32-34 and 240-248","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous IMF mission statement with explicit country-specific assessment and policy objectives.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Abiy Ahmed's government through the finance ministry and central bank officials who met the IMF team; it reflects government policy direction rather than a personal directive.","contrary_evidence":["The same statement says inflation was high and rising.","It also notes the deficit was being financed by central bank advances because external financing was lacking."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
