**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Ethiopian government, ENA, CIA, Reuters/republishers, and related project pages.
- I kept 11 distinct URLs and rejected near-duplicates when they repeated the same macro claim, budget line, or project announcement.
- I rejected several out-of-period retrospectives from 2023-2026 on Ethiopia’s reforms, debt restructuring, and central-bank overhaul because they were useful background but not target-year evidence.
- I rejected generic resource-index pages from the Ministry of Finance and World Bank because they listed documents without adding a material 2022 claim.
- I did not re-fetch local structured datasets, since the local priors already supplied the 2022 macro baseline.
- I treated the conflict-driven macro shock as a constraint to separate inherited conditions from ruler-attributable policy.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct 2022 evidence on whether Abiy personally empowered or overruled economic professionals is still thin.
- Direct anti-corruption, anti-capture, and SOE-governance findings specific to 2022 are limited; most of the evidence is indirect through reform language.
- More exact budget execution and household-distribution data would help, especially by region, class, and gender.
- Better 2022 evidence on whether reforms translated into jobs, price relief, and poverty reduction would materially strengthen the file.
